    "narrative": "As we approach the final stretch of the 2026 season, Ghost Ride the WHIP leans heavily into our speed-starved strategy. **Corbin Carroll** (71.81) leads the charge today against Freeland, benefiting from a soft matchup and the favorable hitting environment that elevates his daily ceiling. Joining him in the top tier is **Josh Naylor** (67.99), who draws a prime opportunity to punish Cristian Javier, and **Mickey Moniak** (67.27), whose 14% platoon edge against Soroka makes him an essential play despite a lower lineup placement. **Seiya Suzuki** (66.43) remains a high-floor anchor at the #2 spot against Junk, while **Gabriel Moreno** (66.10) provides high-efficiency output at the catcher position, maximizing the park factor against a vulnerable Freeland.\n\nOur most difficult decision centered on **Isaac Paredes** (50.19), who sat on the bubble all morning. While he offered the most upside of the reserves, his match against Logan Gilbert\u2014a high-skill arm\u2014coupled with the uncertainty of a pending lineup, pushed him just below the threshold required to displace the high-floor production of **Cole Young** or **Jose Caballero**. We are keeping a close watch on **Brice Turang** (47.04); while his elite speed makes him a roster staple, the combination of facing a tier-one arm in Wheeler and a stiff wind blowing in creates a marginal scoring environment that requires us to monitor his early-game plate appearances closely. We are also tracking the health of **Tyler Soderstrom**, who remains sidelined on the IL, while keeping veterans like **Giancarlo Stanton**, **Trevor Story**, **Max Muncy**, **Dalton Rushing**, and **Edwin Arroyo** in reserve as we carefully manage our remaining game caps for the final push. \n\nRisks today are concentrated on the bottom of the order. Both **Jose Caballero** and **Austin Riley** are dealing with lineup-spot penalties; **Austin Riley** (63.68) survives the cut due to his massive 19% SP skill advantage against Williamson, but **Jose Caballero** (50.84) faces a stiff wind and a low #9 spot, making him our most volatile start. Owners should note that **Griffin Conine** and **Travis Bazzana** were excluded from the primary lineup strictly for falling beneath the 40.0 efficiency floor, as we prioritize maximizing our remaining capacity on players with the clearest paths to runs and stolen bases.",
